   "l": "Do not exalt the worthy,\nand so make the people not contend;\ndo not prize goods hard to come by,\nand so make the people not turn thief;\ndo not display what can be desired,\nand so make the people's hearts not fall into disorder.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Exalt no one for his ability, and the people will not contend; prize no goods that are hard to come by, and the people will not turn thief; put nothing desirable on show, and the people's hearts will stay untroubled.",
   "n": "使 is the causative, and it builds what is called a pivot: in 使民不爭, 民 'the people' is at once the object of 使 'cause' and the subject of 爭 'contend' — 'cause the people not to contend'. It is one of the commonest constructions in the language. 難得之貨 works like chapter 2's 不言之教: 難得 'hard to get' is a verb phrase, and 之 hangs it on 貨 'goods' as a modifier. 可欲 is exactly the 可道 of chapter 1 — 可 plus a verb reads passively, 'what can be desired'. And 民心 is two nouns side by side with nothing between them: 'the people's hearts'. One genuine crux: 見 read xiàn is 'to display, to put on show', which matches the parallel with 'exalt' and 'prize', both of them things the ruler does; read jiàn it is merely 'to see', and the line becomes advice to the ruler's own eye. We take the first and say so.",
   "p": true
  },
